 log; there was no refusal for missing logger stamps (read_log refuses only if neither Date nor created). So no old refusal was replaced by the flag. The flag is purely additive. Good.\n\nplumbline_job.py:\n- The restructure moves `log_row(closed)` after custody+verify. Before, close row was written before custody emit. Now stricter.\n- Hmm wait, actually — careful: in the OLD code, the custody emit ran AFTER `log_row(\"closed\")` and if it failed, return cc (non-zero) with \"CLOSED, BUT...\". In the NEW code, custody emit runs before the closed row; if emit fails, return cc — no closed row. So a close that used to \"succeed\" (with a closed row) but had a failing custody record now doesn't write the closed row. Stricter. Fine.\n- Verifier runs only when terminal_state in TERMINALS (after successful emit). If not terminal, no custody/verify — fine (no record to verify).\n\n- `do_custody`: emits then verifies; on verify failure prints NOT CLOSED and returns vc. Note: \"NOT CLOSED\" in the custody command is a bit odd wording but fine.\n\nNow the potential planted violations. Let me re-read the verifier's `hand_time_set` vs the emitter's `hand` marking for consistency... Not needed for the rule.\n\nLet me reconsider `check_hand_times`'s early return. Actually, think about what \"no verifier failure is skipped when a field is absent\" means concretely. I think it means: e.g., if `x-plumbline.spend` is absent from the record, the verifier must not skip the spend check — it must fail. check_spend handles that (failure). If `hand_times` is absent... check_hand_times returns no failures. Is that \"a verifier failure skipped when a field is absent\"? The failure in question would be \"hand-time-unflagged\" (hand times used but not flagged). If hand_times is absent AND hand times were used, the verifier misses it — unless schema catches. Hmm.\n\nBut actually, wait: would the emitter ever omit hand_times while using hand times? No. The verifier is checking records the emitter