h the bounding commands (commands that stamp the bounds themselves), not typed in.\" The doc's line 142-146 says exactly this and lists dispose/relay/amendment. Line 106 lists frozenAt, approvals[].at, disposition.at as \"typed, or better logged live\". Note: `bar.frozenAt` and `approvals[].at` are NOT \"times reported after the fact\" in the doc's framing — they're stamped live (freeze/approve before maker's first call). The bounding-commands paragraph doesn't cover them. Hmm, but they ARE hand-entered if typed. Point 1 covers them (refused by default). Point 4 covers disposition.at (Owner's decision) — a reported time.\n\nSo:\n- Line 106 vs point 1: implies typed frozenAt/approvals.at/disposition.at accepted by default (refused only on live-log mismatch) → opposite of \"refused by default\".\n- Line 106 vs point 4: implies disposition.at (a reported time) can be typed in → opposite of \"not typed in\".\n\nAnd line 96: \"The emitter no longer trusts a typed time... when a log can say otherwise\" — implies when no log, typed is trusted. Marginal.\n\nHmm, hmm. OK, let me try yet another angle: maybe I'm wrong that the hand-time regime applies to approvals[].at and bar.frozenAt in a way that contradicts line 106. Let me check: is `bar.frozenAt` typically typed by the user, or written by the `freeze` command? Line 380-382: \"Freeze, approval, provider pins, disposition and the batch's own events... Stamp each at the moment it happens; the tool writes it into `inputs/custody.json` and appends it to `inputs/custody-events.jsonl`\". So freeze/approve/dispose write into custody.json. The \"typed\" path in line 106 = the user writes these values into custody.json themselves (before running the tool, or instead of). Under the new regime, that's inputs-no-ledger → refused by default.\n\nHmm wait, actually — hmm, one more consideration. What if the user types frozenAt into custody.json AND runs `freeze`? Then there IS a ledger line behind it (tool-stamped). Not a hand time. Th