gainst the log-derived value (for server-header/response-created kinds). Wait — that means for server-header hand times, the verifier does check the time against the log (it recomputes the server time from the log and requires it flagged). Is that \"verifying\" the hand time? Hmm. The hand time for server-header IS a log time (from the response Date header) but not corroborated by a logger sent_at/received_at. The verifier recomputes it from the log — so it verifies the flag matches the log's server time. The TIME is verified to be what the log says, but the log's server time itself is not corroborated by send/receive stamps. So the flagging is verified; the time's trustworthiness is not established (no independent corroboration).\n\nDoes the document present flagged times as trustworthy? Line 134: \"The switch flags hand times and never verifies them.\" That's explicit: the switch never verifies. The verifier's checks are about flag completeness/honesty. The record's presentation: hand_times[] labeled as hand times with sources. I think point 2 is fine.\n\nPoint 5: \"The `x-plumbline` facts are enforced only by Plumbline's own verifier; the standard custody `check.py` ignores them.\" New lines 8-12, 53-63 state this. Any line implying check.py validates x-plumbline? Line 82: schema kind enforces x-plumbline against schema — that's the verifier (the kinds table is the verifier's). Line 71: exit 0 \"OK at both layers\". Fine. Line 542: drift check re schemas — `schemas/custody-record.schema.json` derivation; not about check.py validating x-plumbline. Fine.\n\nPoint 3: any line implying a relayed approval counts? Line 415: \"the emitter never counts a relay as an approval\". Line 87, 148-150, 185. Fine.\n\nPoint 1: any NEW line implying hand times accepted by default? No — the new lines are emphatic.\n\nPoint 4: any NEW line implying typed-in accepted? No.\n\nSo within new lines: no contradictions. The only candidate remains unmarked line 106 (and 96 marginally).\n\nH