          {
            "role": "user",
            "content": "You are checking a non-normative field guide for people who run E-DI jobs, `docs/PLUMBLINE-ORCHESTRATOR-FIELD-GUIDE.md`, for faithfulness.\n\nQUESTION. Does the document CONFORM to the rule below?\n\nRULE. The document must not contradict, weaken or misstate any of the following, and must not claim to override the doctrine:\nA. The Owner's decisions of 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT:\n   A1. Repeat-failure tripwire: when the same Maker failure class occurs 3 times (exactly three, not \"a few\" or another number), the Maker stops retrying and the request is changed (or reported back). Maker attempts are still not iterations.\n   A2. Spend: the job keeps the shared API key, protected by the harness's pre-call spend guard; because the key is shared, key-usage reconciliation is only an upper bound on the job's spend. The document must not recommend or require a per-job key as the rule.\n   A3. The cross-family pre-read is optional, decided per job on the approval sheet (not mandatory, not dropped).\nB. Standing rules:\n   B1. finish_reason `length` with empty content is its own failure class: stop at the first one and change the request; do not retry it as is.\n   B2. No throttling: max output tokens = the endpoint's maximum and no reasoning limit; tasks are sized so they can finish; finish_reason is logged on every call.\n   B3. The cap counts E-DI iterations, not Maker attempts or generations.\n   B4. Handoffs name the command that writes files (not only a check-only command).\n   B5. Handoffs are checked against the standing rules before they are sent.\n   B6. A live test of each model-calling instrument, at the frozen settings, is a freeze gate.\n   B7. Evidence is write-once (never edited after it is written; corrections go beside it).\n   B8. A relayed approval is a report, not an approval; only the Owner's own words approve.\n   B9. No job work in /tmp.\nC. The doctrine below (evaluatordriven/doctrine DOCTRINE.md v0.1). The guide may add practice, but nothing it says may contradict the doctrine, and it must say that the doctrine wins on conflict.\n\nA statement contradicts a rule if a reader following the document would act against the rule. Leaving out a rule is not a contradiction for this check (presence is checked elsewhere); stating its opposite is.\n\nAnswer in exactly this form and nothing else:\nVERDICT: CONFORMS | DOES_NOT_CONFORM\nLINES: <file>:<line>[, <file>:<line> ...]   (for DOES_NOT_CONFORM, the guide lines that contradict; for CONFORMS, the lines that state A1, A2 and A3)\nREASON: <one or two sentences naming the rule letter and number>\n\nREFERENCE: DOCTRINE.md (not the document under review)\n----- BEGIN DOCTRINE -----\n# Evaluator Driven\n\n**E-DI: Evaluator-Driven Iteration. A doctrine for delegating work so that quality survives.**\n\nVersion 0.1 · ratified 2026-10-25 · Steve Moitozo II · Licence: CC BY 4.0\n\n---\n\n## 0. In one paragraph\n\n**Can I trust our name to this work?** A decision-maker asks that before the company's name goes on work an AI agent produced. A practitioner asks the same of their own name. Evaluator Driven exists so the honest and confident answer\ncan be yes.\n\nWork made by an agent, a model or a person is trusted only as far as it has been judged. Evaluator-Driven work is assessed against a standard that was set before the work began: the bar.\nIt's judged by a qualified Evaluator whose ability to properly judge work has been tested AND who did not\nproduce the work itself. And the record of the work can be checked by anyone who needs to audit it. The evaluation drives the\nwork. It’s not simply paperwork after the fact.\nWhen the work falls short, the Evaluator reports the deficiencies, with proof. The Maker then revises\nagainst exactly that. The Orchestrator ensures the loop stops in a named way, within bounds set in advance, and that the person accountable for the outcome\nkeeps the decisions only they can make. And their decision gets written beside the verdict, never over it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why this exists\n\n**Who it is for.** Three readers:\n\n- the **decision-maker**, who sanctions handing a kind of work to AI and must put the company's name on the result;\n- the **practitioner**, who already uses AI and must put their own name on what it helped make;\n- **the practitioner's AI agent**, which needs a published standard to work to, not its own improvisation.\n\nTwo parties are not readers but **authorities**: the **Buyer**, whose fitness test the work must pass,\nand the **Subject-matter Expert**, whose quality test it must pass.\nTheir standard of “well done” is the one that counts. ⟨Owner, 2026-10-08⟩\n\n**The pain is one defect with three faces: quality that cannot be accounted for.** ⟨Owner, 2026-10-08⟩\n\n- **The signature.** You cannot put a name on work whose quality you cannot account for.\n- **Guilt.** The practitioner got a good result and cannot say what they did to get it. It feels as if the\n  AI produced quality by accident, so they keep quiet about how the work was made.\n- **Delegation that does not survive.** A method that lives in one person's head dies when it is handed\n  to a colleague, a team or an agent. Nobody needs a standard to do their own work; you need one the\n  moment somebody else has to do it too.\n\n**The decision-maker's question is a comparison.** Not \"is AI good?\" but \"will the result be at least as\nacceptable as what it replaces?\" And the decision-maker wants to see that for themselves, not take it on faith.\nEvaluator Driven answers it the only honest way. Hold the work to the bar you would hold your people's\nwork to: the same Subject-matter Expert's standard, the same Buyer's test. Set that bar before the work begins and have the work judged by an Evaluator who did not make it. Work that passes has been judged to meet that bar, and the record lets\nyou check it yourself. Work that does not goes back to the Maker or comes to you, the Owner. ⟨Owner, 2026-10-08; evaluatordriven.com⟩\n\n**The acceptance principle.** In the Owner's words:\n\n> If the work passes the quality test of a subject matter expert and the fitness test of an end-user,\n> shouldn't it be acceptable regardless of the tools used to produce it?\n\nThat is the stricter claim, not the softer one. A standard that judged only methods could be satisfied by\nusing approved tools badly. A standard that judges the work against named tests leaves nothing to\nhide behind, and makes admitting the tool a detail rather than a confession. ⟨Owner, 2026-10-08⟩\n\n**The big bet.** The same job, run inside a well-managed and well-judged loop, beats the same job run without one. The corollary, if the bet holds, is what makes it matter: quality comes from the judging and detailed feedback more than from the size of the Maker.\nSo, a modest, cheap, even locally hosted model inside the loop can produce work that passes the bar. This\nis why Evaluator Driven exists. §8 sets out the evidence so far, and what would settle it.\n⟨Mandate §8, B.1.5; Owner, 2026-10-24⟩\n\n**Why now.** Generation used to be the expensive step, so outputs were checked carefully. Now producing work is close to free, and the binding constraint is the judging. The work is still checked. What changes is that the checking itself can now be delegated with confidence, once it meets a standard of its own: a bar set first, an Evaluator tested before it serves, and a record anyone can audit. The person who gets quality from agents is the one who\ninvests more in the Evaluator and loop discipline than in the Maker. Delegation fails in known ways. When \"done\" and \"good\" are unclear, even human delegation suffers. The work cannot be checked fairly. The worker lacks the skill. The feedback is late or useless.\nOr the work is graded by its Maker, or by judges who know neither the Buyer's need nor what masterful\nwork looks like.\n⟨Primer, \"Why this exists\"; Mandate P2; Owner, 2026-10-25⟩\n\n**The practice is old; the name is new.** Good editors, managers and craftsmen have always defined done\nbefore handing off. They checked the work against the standard, not the worker's account of it. They sent\nit back with specific feedback, and kept the final call with the person whose name is on it. Agents work\nwithout you in the room, so your standards survive your absence only if they are written down. ⟨Primer⟩\n\n**Neighbouring methods cover part of the ground.** A specification tells an agent what to build; it does\nnot show that the agent built it. A proof covers what a formal specification can state; most work (prose,\ntranslation, design, data, judgement calls) cannot be proved. Evaluator Driven is for the rest.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The grounding axiom: the Evaluator is a proxy, never a principal\n\nBefore any mechanics, one question governs everything:\n\n> **Who is buying this output, and which master of the craft approved the bar?**\n\nQuality is not a platonic property a rubric can point at. It lives with two authorities, and the bar sits\nwhere they meet:\n\n- the **Buyer** defines fitness for purpose: does this solve my problem, within my constraints?\n- the **Specialist**, the subject-matter expert, defines excellence: would a master of the craft accept\n  this?\n\nThey can pull against each other, and judgement must hold both. **The bar is the intersection of the Buyer's need and\nthe Specialist's standard.** ⟨Primer, grounding axiom; Playbook, seats⟩\n\nSo an Evaluator holds authority on loan. Its standard must carry provenance to both, written down as four\nfacts:\n\n1. who the Buyer is;\n2. what they need, and which constraints they named;\n3. whose standard of excellence is borrowed;\n4. where that standard lives, versioned.\n\nA rubric that cannot answer those four is a preference wearing a score. **No provenance, no authority.** ⟨Primer; Instrument rule 1⟩\n\nTwo consequences follow. **You can delegate the doing; you can never delegate responsibility for the\nstandard.** And **the doctrine improves how well work meets its frame; it does not set the frame.** Values,\npriorities, risk appetite and taste belong to the Owner. ⟨Primer; Mandate X1–X3⟩\n\n---\n\n## 3. The seats\n\nA seat is a responsibility, not a person. One person or model may hold several seats if that is\ndisclosed, with one exception that is never waived: **the Maker never judges its own work.**\n\n| Seat | Does | Never does |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Owner** | Owns the outcome. Ratifies the frame. Sets values, priority and risk appetite. Decides what the loop cannot. May override a verdict, recorded as a disposition beside it. | Rewrites a verdict. |\n| **Architect** | Defines done and good, writes the standard, sets the thresholds and bounds, and builds the instruments. | Writes or tunes the standard after seeing a draft. |\n| **Buyer** | Is the one whose problem is being solved. States the need and the constraints. | — |\n| **Specialist** | Supplies the standard of excellence for the craft. | — |\n| **Maker** | Produces the work and revises it against classified feedback. | Judges its own output, or produces the observation it is judged by. |\n| **Evaluator** | Judges the work against the frozen standard, through a channel the Maker did not produce, and returns a structured verdict. | Produces or repairs the work. |\n| **Orchestrator** | Runs the loop: dispatch, feedback, bounds, the record. | Writes the work or scores it. |\n\n⟨Playbook §6; Primer, \"The seats, named\"; Mandate X4⟩\n\n**Two forms of the Evaluator seat** have earned names in practice:\n\n- **The adjudicator** is an Evaluator who rules on a disagreement between Evaluators. It reads the work\n  itself (the code, the text). An adjudicator never resolves a split by averaging.\n- **The test author** writes an independent channel: a test, a planted case, a task for a reader who has\n  never seen the work. The test author is not the Maker and does not see the Maker's reasoning.\n\n⟨practice⟩\n\n**On families of models.** Seats may share an engine, a vendor or a model family. What may not happen is\nan Evaluator seat biased toward the Maker's family. Two models from one family tend to share blind spots, and an Evaluator that shares the Maker's blind spots is the Maker grading itself by another route. So:\n\n- at least one Evaluator differs from the Maker in model family;\n- on every dimension, the Maker's family holds no more than half of the Evaluators;\n- every seat's holder is disclosed, and an undisclosed model family is read the least favourable way: as the Maker's.\n\n⟨custody record v0.9.6, CL2-3; practice⟩\n\n---\n\n## 4. Principles\n\n1. **The bar comes first.** Done and good exist before the first draft. A definition written after seeing a draft is an anchor on the draft, not a standard. For existing work (Entry B), the bar comes before the judging instead, and declaration order stands in for blindness. ⟨Mandate M1, M1.1; Primer, core sequence⟩\n\n2. **Build the Evaluator before the Maker produces, and show it catching a bad case.** Write the instrument, run it on a case it must fail (the work's own real failure if there is one, otherwise a deliberately bad case), then freeze and hash it. If you have never seen the instrument say no, its yes means nothing. ⟨Instrument A2, rule 3, Entry B's thirteenth rule⟩\n\n3. **The Maker never grades its own work, and never produces the observation it's graded on.** The\n   Evaluator sees the work. The observation comes through a channel the Maker did not produce and cannot\n   easily fake. Examples: tests the author did not write; a back-translation by a reader who never saw the\n   source; a stranger following the instructions cold. ⟨Mandate M2; Primer, move 2; Instrument rule 2⟩\n\n4. **Anchor to ground truth, and name how hard it is.** Judge against the source and known-good\n   references, never the Maker's working copy. Ground truth comes in kinds: an invariant, a document of\n   record, a prior. No dimension may claim more certainty than the channel that carries it. ⟨Primer, move 2;\n   Instrument rule 8⟩\n\n5. **Judges are tested too.** Every Evaluator, in every batch, reads at least one case that should pass and\n   one that should fail. The model serving a seat is part of the instrument, and its strictness,\n   stability and cost are measured, not assumed. A verdict rests on at least two scored runs; one run is a\n   sample. A judge that is never tested can name a defect and then excuse it, or report checking text that is not there; planted cases are how you find out. Put cheap deterministic checks (length,\n   structure, required elements) in front of the judge: they catch what a lenient judge waves through.\n   And measure each Evaluator's spread on identical input: a reading that changes between identical runs is\n   the instrument's noise, so only what holds across runs counts as a finding.\n   ⟨Instrument rule 10, Entry B's thirteenth rule; Contracts §10.6; practice⟩\n\n6. **Disagreement is information; never average it away.** A scored pass needs both an average threshold and a floor on every dimension, because an average alone lets a fatal defect hide. Readings from\n   different Evaluators are never averaged: where they agree within the channel's measured spread, the\n   agreement is the reading; where they split, the split is shown and adjudicated. A disagreement between\n   the work and the standard is traced to its cause, never written off as a bad Maker. Either the work missed the\n   standard, the standard moved, the dimension was never authorized, or the frame itself is at fault.\n   ⟨Contracts §3, §10.6; Instrument rule 4; practice⟩\n\n7. **Feedback carries the shortfall, always; a remedy only with real insight.** Say how the work falls\n   short and quote the evidence. Offer a remedy where the Evaluator genuinely sees one. An invented remedy is\n   worse than none; a measured remedy withheld is also a defect. A remedy points the way; it is never the fix. The Evaluator does not write the revision, and the Maker owns how the shortfall is fixed. An Evaluator that writes the fix grades its own work on the next round. A score with no observation behind it is not\n   a score. And \"could not observe\" is a different fact from \"found missing\": what an Evaluator cannot see\n   is recorded as unobserved, never as absent. ⟨Mandate M3; Instrument rule 3; Contracts §5; practice⟩\n\n8. **Feedback must be able to change the next version, or do not loop.** If the Maker cannot act on the\n   judgement, iteration is theatre. Identical outputs mean the influence channel is dormant, not weak. A\n   capability gap escalates; it is not retried. ⟨Mandate M3.6, M4; Primer, move 3; Instrument rule 5⟩\n\n9. **Inside a loop, a change answers only what the Evaluator reported.** Everything else waits for the next\n   bar. Carry the best work forward: a revision replaces the incumbent only if it is better on at least one\n   dimension and worse on none. A trade between dimensions is the Owner's call, never the loop's.\n   ⟨Primer, worked example; Instrument rule 12⟩\n\n10. **Bound the loop, and name every exit.** A cap, a budget and a time limit are supplied, never defaulted,\n    and bind while the run proceeds. Every path ends in a named state. ⟨Mandate M5; Instrument rule 6⟩\n\n11. **Escalate without interrogation.** What can be found out, the agents find out. Values, priority, risk\n    and the Buyer's intent go to the Owner, with what was tried, why no observable answer exists, and the\n    options with a recommendation. A bare question is work pushed back, not an escalation. **The Owner's\n    attention is a budget, and it is measured.** There are three signals, each with a target of zero. The\n    same kind of approval asked again and again. Choices put without a recommendation. Answers the Owner has\n    to ask to have re-explained. A system that leaves its Owner feeling worked by the machine has failed, however good\n    its output. ⟨Mandate M6, F5; Instrument, \"Report where the frame failed\"; practice⟩\n\n12. **Evidence or it did not happen.** Every claim cites a file, kept by its content hash. Notes are written\n    from what the instruments produced, never from what someone intended. Keep the instrument that\n    accepted the work, and the record, with the work: committed beside it, not in local scratch. Pin every\n    standard by hash or commit, never by a version name alone; a version number cannot be checked. Figures,\n    hashes and record fields are printed by the tool that produced them, never retyped by hand.\n    ⟨Mandate M2, M5, F6; Instrument rule 10; practice⟩\n\n13. **Proportion.** The instrument is as heavy as the risk and no heavier. A smaller instrument is a recorded\n    choice. It is never partial: it may not skip a must-ask value, a terminal state or a checked line.\n    ⟨Mandate M7; Instrument rule 11⟩\n\n14. **The level of delegation changes who decides, never what must be supplied.** Declare the rung (do\n    exactly this; research and report; research and recommend; decide and inform; act). No rung waives a\n    must-ask. ⟨Mandate M8; Instrument rule 9⟩\n\n---\n\n## 5. The loop\n\n**Four moves, each one an answer for the person who signs:** ⟨Owner, 2026-10-08⟩\n\n| Move | What it lets the signer say |\n|---|---|\n| **Set the bar** | I decided what \"good\" and \"done\" would mean before I saw the result. |\n| **Produce against it** | The method is written down, so it can happen again, in someone else's hands. |\n| **Evaluate independently** | Somebody who did not make the output checked it. |\n| **Keep the record** | My name is attached, and so is everything it rests on. |\n\nBehind them sits the core sequence: define quality and done, build the Evaluator, build the Maker, then run the loop. Every loop that works runs in that order. You cannot evaluate what you have not defined. ⟨Primer, core sequence⟩ And a\ndefinition written after seeing a draft is anchored on the draft.\n\n### Two entries\n\n**Commissioned work (Entry A)** does not exist yet. The standard is written first, so it cannot have been\ntuned to the artifact. Before generating, the Maker states the win and the done rule back in its own\nwords: the cheapest defect filter there is. ⟨Instrument, Entry A⟩\n\n**Existing work (Entry B, retrofit)** is already made, perhaps already in use. You cannot be blind to an\nanswer that exists, so declaration order substitutes for blindness. First collect the work's own standard:\nits docs, tests and claims. Then declare the frame you would hold it to now, and freeze the instrument.\nOnly then read anything that could reveal a defect. The record says which reads came before the freeze.\nThe past gets a census, not a verdict, and thresholds apply forward. The richest finding is usually the\nwork contradicting itself: claims made but not implemented, and behaviour implemented but not claimed.\n⟨Instrument, Entry B⟩\n\n### One turn of the loop\n\n1. **Freeze.** The bar, the instruments and the bounds are fixed and hashed. The work starts after.\n2. **Make.** The Maker produces a version.\n3. **Observe.** Independent channels produce observations the Maker did not touch.\n4. **Judge.** Evaluator(s) score or read each dimension, each score with its quoted observation, alongside\n   their controls. The verdict is a structure, not prose: dimensions, violations (each classified, each\n   quoting its evidence), and a recommendation of accept, regenerate, needs owner, or reject.\n5. **Decide the step.** Pass the rule: stop. Fall short: send the classified shortfall back to the Maker\n   as constraints. Nothing else goes back.\n6. **Revise and repeat**, within the bounds.\n\n⟨Primer, \"The evaluator's contract\", \"The run packet\"; Example⟩\n\n### The exits\n\nA job ends in one of three states: **accepted**, **needs owner**, or **rejected**.\n\n- **Accepted:** the work passed the rule fixed before it began.\n- **Needs owner:** the cap was reached without a pass while the work stayed above the reject floor, or an escalation fired, or the instrument failed. Below the cap, such work goes back to the Maker. The **best version** the job produced goes to the Owner, never to the bin.\n- **Rejected:** below the reject floor. Work not yet in use is discarded. Work already in use is never\n  discarded by the loop; the verdict stands and what to do is the Owner's call.\n\nBeside the state, the record carries the outcome that explains it. For example:\n\n- *census*: readings only, because no Specialist was named when the job began, so there is no bar to score against; every readable dimension must conform, or the job goes to the Owner;\n- *no observation*: the instrument failed. That is a finding about the instrument, never a zero for the\n  work;\n- *no artifact*: the Maker produced nothing.\n\nThere is no state in which a loop quietly ends. ⟨Mandate M5.2; Instrument, Exits⟩\n\n**Verdict and disposition are separate acts.** The Owner may ship a rejected piece or shelve an accepted\none. The verdict stays as the loop produced it; the decision is written beside it. ⟨Mandate X4;\nInstrument rule 7⟩\n\n### Where the human sits\n\nOn well-trodden ground, where an Evaluator has a validation record and calibrated thresholds, the loop\nruns without a person and exceptions surface as *needs owner*. On novel ground (a new domain, new stakes, a\nfirst run), a person stays in every turn until the proxy earns trust. The mistake is not keeping a human too\nlong; it is removing one before the proxy has proved itself. This is human-at-the-helm, not just human-in-the-loop: the person sets the bar before the work begins, keeps the decisions only they can make, and decides when the loop has earned the right to run without them. ⟨Primer, \"The human stays terminal\"⟩\n\n---\n\n## 6. Qualifying the seats\n\nSeat no one on reputation, including the Architect.\n\n- **Test every seat on planted cases before it serves.** A Maker shows it can generate and, separately,\n  revise; an Evaluator shows it can fail what it judges; an Orchestrator shows it stops when the runbook\n  says stop. The Architect's seat is not yet tested this way; that gap is open. ⟨Instrument rule 10; practice⟩\n- **Check every instrument against a valid alternative.** Before an instrument scores anything, run it on a\n  correct solution built differently from the one its author had in mind. An instrument that fails a valid\n  alternative is measuring the author's design, not the bar. ⟨practice⟩\n- **Have someone independent pre-read the instruments.** The Architect writes the runbook and the scorer;\n  an adjudicator reads both against the bar before any work starts. Where the instrument and the bar\n  differ, the bar governs. ⟨practice⟩\n- **Pin instruments before use**, and keep them by hash. A rerun with the same rubric and a different\n  runner is a different instrument. ⟨Instrument rule 10⟩\n\nBuilding the custody record showed why. In v0.9.5, two of the Architect's scoring tools scored a preferred design rather than the rules. In v0.9.6 it happened again: the Orchestrator found a tool that would have failed a valid design the brief allowed, and the work was scored again with a corrected tool, both scorings kept. The rules on valid alternatives and independent pre-reads exist to catch that before anything is scored. ⟨custody record v0.9.6: BAR-v0.9.6, theme; qualification-v0.9.6/QUAL-AMENDMENT-1⟩\n\n---\n\n## 7. The record\n\nThe record is what lets a person sign with integrity and confidence: it shows what the work was measured against, who checked it\nindependently, and who accepted it. A loop that leaves no record cannot be trusted by anyone who was not in the room when it was made. The record is how the doctrine reaches someone who wasn’t there to witness the work. It shows what the bar was and who held which seat. It\nshows what each Evaluator saw, how they disagreed, what the Maker changed, and how the job ended. Accepted work that cannot be reconstructed from\nits records counts as a failure of the doctrine (§8, F6). ⟨Mandate M5, F6⟩\n\nThe interoperable form of that record is the **Evaluator Driven custody record**, `custody/v1`: a\nhash-chained log and an in-toto predicate, with conformance levels a checker can verify. It is a separate,\nneutral specification with its own repository. It is still a candidate: its v0.9.6 stranger test did not pass, and no product loop yet emits `custody/v1`. ⟨custody record v0.9.6⟩ The record states facts; this doctrine says\nwhich facts matter and why. **The doctrine never redefines the record's terms**, and the record never\ndepends on the doctrine. A job run under another method can still produce a valid record. The record will\nshow how far that job met the practices above.\n\n---\n\n## 8. How you would know it failed\n\nThe doctrine names its own failure signals, so it can be judged by them. ⟨Mandate F1–F6⟩\n\n- **F1.** Work ships with no Buyer in the record who asked for it.\n- **F2.** Judging costs more than the errors it catches (on a fair comparison).\n- **F3.** Work scored above the bar is later rejected by the named Buyer or Specialist, and the record\n  cannot say which dimension failed.\n- **F4.** Two honest runs of the same setup disagree by more than the instrument's normal variation.\n- **F5.** The Owner is asked questions the system could have answered itself. Or the Owner is forced to carry an attention cost the system could have spared: repeated approvals of the same kind, choices without a\n  recommendation, answers that need re-explaining. ⟨Mandate F5; practice⟩\n- **F6.** Accepted work cannot be reconstructed from its records.\n\n**What is claimed is repeatability, not scale.** The bar, the instruments and the record exist so a result\ncan be reproduced by someone else. Nothing yet supports a claim that the method scales, so none is made. Repeatability is designed in, not yet shown: no result here is known to have been reproduced by someone else.\n⟨Owner, 2026-10-08⟩\n\n**The evidence for the big bet, so far.** ⟨Mandate §8, B.1.5; evidence/software-defect-pairs.md; evaluation records⟩\n\n| Case | Maker | What was compared | Result |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A translation system for low-resource languages | Code: a 9B model, hosted locally, writing to tests written first by a cloud-hosted model. Translation: a 1.3B model, hosted locally | The build: the loop only. The judging: an audit against planted defects, run independently of the judges under test | 900 of 911 tests pass offline with a dummy key set; 10 of the 11 failures are a real broken integrity gate. Spanish content made in one pass, outside the loop, carried real grammar errors in 3 of 100 units, which its review missed. The production judge, tested on planted defects, accepted 9 of 9 in its production mode; a simple structure check caught 6 of the 9, and one escaped every automated check. On 2026-09-06, two Lingala speakers read the Lingala output and accepted it: a first-language speaker reviewed 10 days of devotional content, and a second-language speaker read 5 (a retrospective report: which days, and how they read, are not recorded). One project, not a paired comparison |\n| Seven real software defects in two web applications | One locally hosted 35B-class model, the same on both arms | One pass on all seven; the loop only on the 3 the pass missed. An executable probe as the pass rule (in one row a failing held test also counted as a miss), and a second Evaluator from another family agreeing on all seven | One pass fixed 4. Of the 3 it missed, the loop fixed 2 and missed 1. The Owner named this *a* comparator for software defect resolution, not *the* comparator |\n| One live defect in a sign-in code endpoint | A 4B model, hosted locally | One pass versus the loop, with a second Evaluator agreeing | One pass missed; the loop was accepted on its fourth generation. Earlier loops on this defect, at temperature 0, did not reach acceptance and are not counted. Loop judging cost under half a cent, or $0.0139 with the second Evaluator's check. A hosted model fixed the same defect in one pass for $0.0140, with no second check: which is cheaper depends on what is counted. The Owner reports the defect cost about $20 in production; whether judging pays for itself (F2) is still open |\n| A mobile devotional app (Android) | A 9B model, hosted locally, writing code to failing tests written first by the Evaluator; a cloud-hosted model from another family reviewed the diffs | The loop only; no run without it | A signed Android release build, with the unit, contract and browser suites recorded green (2026-08-29). The outside review caught 4 serious defects the tests had missed, and each was fixed through the loop. Files were later changed outside the loop, with no gate, and the archived source now fails its own tests: the loop protects only what passes through it. No device test was run, iOS was never built, and the release APK probably lacks its privacy policy. One project, not a paired comparison |\n\n**Three conditions on the bet. The first two were learned the hard way in the translation work, the third in the mobile app.**\n\n1. **The feedback must be able to change the work.** The first translation loop (2026-09-03) gave its\n   translation model feedback that model could not act on. Of 25 jobs, 1 was accepted. Seven Lingala jobs never produced a score; the rest wandered, mostly between 1 and 4, without converging. Nine of 12 Swahili jobs scored exactly 7.8 on both iterations, with the same one-line feedback: consistent with a dormant influence channel. The next day a step was added that rewrote the source in\n   response to the Evaluator's complaints. A different 20-row batch then passed 17 of 20 at a threshold of 7.5, under the same loop's own judge. The main change was a way to act on the judgement. That judge was later found to pass planted defects (condition 2), so the rise shows the loop could act on its judge, not how far quality rose.\n   ⟨Primer, move 3; practice⟩\n2. **The judge must be qualified.** The later audit shows the second: a loop with an untested Evaluator\n   passes defects with confidence. Well-judged is something you measure with planted cases (§6), not something you assume.\n3. **The work must stay inside the loop.** Changes made outside it are neither judged nor recorded. In the\n   mobile app, files were later changed with no gate, and the archived source now fails its own tests. ⟨mobile-app review, 2026-10-24⟩\n\nOnly the software-defect pair and the Lingala reading are published (evidence/). The other translation and mobile-app records are the Owner's and not yet published; read those rows as his report.\n\nThe bet is on a loop that meets all three.\n\n**What would settle it:** paired jobs in more than one domain. Each pair keeps the same Buyer, the same\nstandard and the same class of work, and runs once with the loop and once without. Each is published with\nits record, and the results are reported as a rate that counts the misses as well as the wins, with outcomes beyond the pass: use, early detection and cost. Until then the bet is supported in software, shown working but not compared in translation, and not yet established across domains. This document says so rather than borrowing\nauthority it has not earned. ⟨Mandate B.1.5⟩\n\n---\n\n## 9. What this doctrine is not\n\n- **Not a method for making things.** Make whatever products you like with whatever tools you like: by specification, by proof, by hand, by agent. Evaluator Driven governs how the work is judged, fed back and carried, and so it shapes the loop the Maker works in.\n- **Not a source of values.** It does not set values, priorities, risk appetite or taste; the Owner does,\n  and the doctrine then scores against that standard. ⟨Mandate X1–X3⟩\n- **Not a replacement for the Owner's override**, which is recorded beside the verdict, never over it.\n  ⟨Mandate X4⟩\n- **Not a product.** It is a set of rules you adopt, not a product you buy. ⟨evaluatordriven.com⟩\n- **Not certification.** A record shows how a judgement was made, not that the work is correct. ⟨custody record, SPEC §1⟩\n- **Not finished.** It is versioned, and it changes when a job shows that it must, not before. No rule\n  without a job that needed it. ⟨Instrument, Refusals⟩\n\n---\n\n## 10. Lineage\n\n**Origin.** The doctrine was first written down on 2026-08-31 as \"Evaluator Driven Iteration\": define\nquality first, build the evaluator before the generator, close the loop. ⟨Primer, references⟩ It grew into a corpus of seven weight-bearing documents: a quickstart, a playbook, an operating instrument, machine contracts, a mandate, a worked example and a guide for machines. A primer teaches it but is not weight-bearing. The Owner ratified each document and its later amendments, and the set is held at commit `a48a464`. The Primer was itself revised under the method: Evaluators from five model families, none of them the reviser, scoring against an instrument written before the revision existed. The corpus is private; these facts are the Owner's record of it. ⟨Playbook, load map; Primer, revision note v1.1⟩\n\n**This articulation.** Version 0.1 restates the corpus for readers who will never open it. It adds the\nlessons of the custody-record releases v0.9.4 to v0.9.6, marked ⟨practice⟩ above. Its rules: test Evaluators with planted cases in every batch; never average disagreements; check instruments against valid alternatives and pre-read them before use; keep evidence by hash, and write notes from output, not intent. Those\nreleases were themselves run under this doctrine, and their records are public: https://github.com/smoitozo/evaluatordriven, tags v0.9.4 to v0.9.6. One rule departs from the\ncorpus on purpose. The corpus barred the Maker's family from the Evaluator seat outright. This version adopts the custody record's principle: the seat must not be biased toward the Maker's family (§3). The record's rule, CL2-3, also requires a different endpoint.\n\n**What it stands on.** Covey's stewardship delegation (desired results, guidelines, resources,\naccountability, consequences). Hyatt's five levels of delegation. The writer–reviewer split in agent\nworkflows. The finding that models correcting themselves with only internal reasoning improve little or\nget worse, while structured external feedback helps (Gou et al., *CRITIC*, 2023). The constitutional\nform: a written, versioned standard, critiques that cite the clause they enforce, and revisions that\nanswer the citation. Evaluation-driven development: a scorecard before optimization, dimensions rather\nthan a pooled average. Teach-back from clinical practice: the person repeats the instruction in their own\nwords, because \"do you understand?\" reliably gets a yes. ⟨Instrument, \"What we stand on\"; Primer, move 1⟩\n\n**Applied to itself.** This document had to pass the gate it describes. The Architect drafted it for the Owner's edit; a reader from another model family checked it for claims its evidence does not support; the Owner ratified it on 2026-10-25.\n\n---\n\n*Evaluator Driven, v0.1. © 2026 Steve Moitozo II. Licensed under CC BY 4.0.\nevaluatordriven.com*\n\n----- END DOCTRINE -----\n\nDOCUMENT:\n=== FILE docs/PLUMBLINE-ORCHESTRATOR-FIELD-GUIDE.md (the whole file at the revision, numbered) ===\n    1   # Running a Plumbline job as Orchestrator: a field guide\n    2   \n    3   Status: **non-normative.** This is a working document of practice that has held up, and of the failures that taught it. It is not the doctrine, not the bar and not the custody record. Nothing here overrides any of them, and where this guide and the doctrine disagree, the doctrine wins.\n    4   \n    5   Version of 2026-10-28. Started during the Plumbline job `2026-10-27-custody-emitter-gaps` and revised with the review that came out of the evaluatordriven custody jobs v0.9.6 and v0.9.7 and the later `2026-10-28-doctrine-reader-pointer` job. Passages added from that review are marked **[added]**.\n    6   \n    7   Read this before you open a job. Every rule below has a story behind it, and the story comes from a real job or review where the rule was broken or where its absence cost money, time or a batch. The stories are here so you can reason from the why when you meet a case these rules do not cover. Model, provider and job names are kept as the records carry them; personal names, addresses and key material are not.\n    8   \n    9   ---\n   10   \n   11   ## 1. What you are, and what you are not\n   12   \n   13   You are the **Orchestrator**. You write the bar with the Owner, freeze it, open the job, route work between the seats, apply the pass rule mechanically, keep the record, and bring decisions to the Owner. That is the whole job.\n   14   \n   15   You are **not** the Maker and **not** the Evaluator. You never write the fix, never make the Maker's model calls, never score. If you catch yourself \"just fixing one line\" in the subject, stop: that removes the separation the method exists to prove.\n   16   \n   17   **Relayed approvals are not approvals.** Only the Owner's own words approve anything. Another agent or bot saying \"the Owner said yes\" is a report, not an approval. Record it as relayed, with the time and the channel, and say plainly that you did not witness it.\n   18   \n   19   - *Story:* in the custody-times job the seats' go-aheads were logged as relays, not as witnessed approvals, and the close line could only say the go-ahead happened in the Maker's own chat. In the evaluatordriven jobs one Owner decision in the log could not be tied to any event at all, which is what a relay looks like once the channel is gone.\n   20   \n   21   **Disclose a combined seat.** If one seat writes an instrument and also runs the gate it is judged by, or applies the pass rule to its own instrument's output, say so in the record's `separationGaps`. Separation is the thing the record exists to prove, and a reader has to know when the ruler and the reading sit in one hand.\n   22   \n   23   - *Story:* in the Plumbline jobs the Orchestrator wrote the bar and the instruments (the instrument runner, the batch script, the rubric, the guarded call wrapper), the Evaluator bot ran them, and the Orchestrator applied the pass rule. The record disclosed the Maker operator and the hand-typed ledger lines but not that the instruments and the pass rule sat in one seat. That line goes in from the next job.\n   24   \n   25   ## 2. The words that matter\n   26   \n   27   - **Iteration** = one turn of the loop: the Maker produces, the Evaluator scores, you apply the pass rule. **The cap counts E-DI iterations.** The Owner settled this on 2026-10-28: the three meant three turns of the loop, not three Maker sessions.\n   28   - **Maker-internal attempts** (retries, follow-up calls, regenerations inside one Maker turn) are not iterations. Let the Maker loop inside its money and time budget, subject to the tripwire below.\n   29   - **The tripwire (Owner decision, 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT):** when the **same failure class** comes up **3 times**, the Maker stops retrying and must **change the request**. A different failure class starts its own count. Log every attempt with its class: `finish_reason` = `length` with empty content, a diff or extraction that will not apply, the same test failing, a provider refusal.\n   30     - The tripwire does not cap spend; the budget does. It bounds retries of one kind of failure, and it makes you notice a broken delivery path while you still have budget to fix it.\n   31     - *Story:* in the v0.9.6 job 9 of 10 Maker attempts failed at \"the diff does not apply\", with hunk counts off by one to three, and none failed at a semantic gate. A limit on attempts is what exposed the transport as the problem rather than the model; unlimited retries would have spent the budget on it. Ours: generation 2 returned four complete-but-corrupt diffs, none of which applied, and the fix was to change the delivery contract to whole files.\n   32   - **Write the cap in the bar in one sentence:** \"cap N E-DI iterations; Maker attempts limited by budget, the clock-time bound, and the tripwire.\" A bare count (\"3 generations\", \"3 revisions\") gets read as counting Maker sessions or round-trips.\n   33     - *Story:* our bar said \"cap 3 generations\". Generations 1 and 2 failed for plumbing reasons (a token cap, malformed diffs) before the Evaluator scored anything, and when iteration 1 then failed scoring the bar said the attempts were gone. The Owner had to amend the cap just to allow a second real iteration. The evaluatordriven jobs hit the same thing independently with bars that said \"3 revisions\".\n   34   - **Bar** = the frozen standard: criteria, instruments, pass rule, bounds. Its digest is what the Buyer approves.\n   35   - **Frozen set** = everything listed in `custody/MANIFEST.sha256`. Changing any of it after freeze is a **Buyer amendment**, never a quiet edit.\n   36   \n   37   ## 3. Before you freeze: the checklist\n   38   \n   39   Most of the pain in the first jobs came from things that should have been caught before freeze. Do every item.\n   40   \n   41   1. **No throttling.** No reasoning limit, and max output tokens at the **endpoint max** — look it up on the provider's endpoints page and save a snapshot. This applies to the Maker and to every model inside an Evaluator instrument.\n   42      - *Story 1:* a Maker generation ran under a 24k token cap. The model spent 19.9k thinking and returned nothing usable.\n   43      - *Story 2:* an instrument was frozen with an 800-token cap. All three scoring calls spent about 798 tokens reasoning and returned empty. The batch was void, and the Owner had to approve an amendment to rerun it.\n   44   2. **The endpoint max is necessary, not sufficient: size each task so it finishes inside the maximum.** Split the work into calls that fit with room to spare, and log `finish_reason` on every call together with the reasoning and content token counts, so a starved call shows up in the log instead of in a guess.\n   45      - *Story:* in the evaluatordriven jobs one request asked for 12 records in a single reply. It failed five times with `finish_reason` = `length` at 131,072 tokens, almost all reasoning and no content, at about $0.59 a call — roughly $3 before anyone changed the request. Batching fixed it: four calls of three records each.\n   46      - *Ours:* the guarded harness logged `finish_reason` = `stop` with both token counts on the first iteration, which is how a healthy call looks in the log.\n   47   3. **`finish_reason` = `length` with empty content is its own failure class. Stop at the first one.** Do not wait for the tripwire's third time: the same request at the same maximum gives the same result and the same bill, so an identical retry cannot turn out differently. Change the request — a smaller task, or a split into several calls. A changed request is a new Maker attempt, not an iteration; if the change alters anything in the frozen set, it is an amendment.\n   48      - *Story:* the 12-record call above was retried five times before anyone resized it. Our own 24k Maker cap and the 800-token instrument showed the same signature, reasoning with no content, and the right reading is \"resize\", not \"retry\".\n   49   4. **Run a live test of every model-calling instrument at the exact frozen settings before freezing, and make the live run a freeze gate.** One real call per instrument, at the frozen model, provider pin, max tokens and prompt template; save the request, the reply, `finish_reason` and the cost to a file and cite that file's hash on the checklist. No attached output, no freeze. Dry runs and mocks do not catch a starved model.\n   50      - *Story:* the evaluatordriven jobs dry-ran the deterministic instruments (64 of 64 as expected) but never ran the records splitter live. Five paid calls failed after the freeze. Our 800-token instrument was the same miss. The doctrine-pointer job did it right: both pinned providers were live-tested before freeze, and the output is committed under `custody/livetest/`.\n   51   5. **Write the Maker's output contract into the bar: whole files between markers.** Models write whole files reliably and write unified diffs badly.\n   52      - *Story:* generation 2 returned four complete-but-corrupt diffs, none of which applied. Generation 3 delivered whole files through the extractor and passed on call 2. The bar had said \"patch\", which left the Owner's intent to interpretation.\n   53   6. **The runbook must name the command that writes the files, not only the one that checks them.** Every handoff step that produces bytes names the command that writes them.\n   54      - *Story:* in the doctrine-pointer job the first Maker handoff went from the extractor with its check flag, which writes nothing, straight to \"commit\". The Maker spotted it and ran the same command without the check flag to write the reply verbatim, then committed. That was right, but the runbook should never leave the Maker to guess.\n   55   7. **Check every handoff against the standing rules before you send it.** Handoffs written by a helper drift: a cap on retries, a different commit identity, a temp path.\n   56      - *Story:* in the doctrine-pointer job the first handoff capped Maker retries at one and had the helper commit as the Owner. Both contradict the standing rules — the cap counts E-DI iterations, and the Maker is the author with the Owner as committer. The Orchestrator caught both and overrode them in the relay.\n   57   8. **State the time bound as a clock time**, for example \"ends 2026-10-28 13:15 EDT\", not \"under 10 hours\", and derive every cutoff from it in the handoffs with the arithmetic shown.\n   58      - *Story:* a call-start cutoff and the job's end were mixed up, and nobody could tell which number was the real limit.\n   59   9. **Plan the approval checkpoints against the clock, and against any decision cap.** Every Maker or Evaluator model run needs the Owner's yes. If the Owner is asleep the clock keeps running; if the bar caps decisions, count your planned checkpoints against that cap when you draft the sheet.\n   60      - *Story:* a generation waited overnight for a yes and the job overran its time bound before the call started. Separately, an evaluatordriven bar capped Owner decisions at 10 and the job used 12; a standing ruling for a defined sequence (\"proceed, uncapped, three per session\") saved several asks.\n   61   10. **Enforce spend in the harness, not in prose.** Ceilings written in a handoff are procedural; nothing stops a runaway call. The job keeps the **shared key** behind a pre-call **spend guard**: before each paid call the guard compares spend so far, plus the call's worst case, plus a small reserve, against the bound, and refuses before anything is sent if that would cross it.\n   62       - **Owner decision, 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT:** the job keeps the shared key, guarded before every paid call, rather than taking a per-job key. Reconciliation against key usage is therefore an **upper bound**, not an exact figure, and the record must say so. This guide does not recommend a per-job key; the Owner weighed that option and chose the shared key.\n   63       - What the guard covers: \"spent\" is the larger of the job's own ledger and the key's usage change since the job's baseline; unsettled calls count as spent; only priced model and provider pairs can be called; it refuses when key usage cannot be read.\n   64       - What it does not cover, so say it in the record: spend that bypasses the guard is counted after the fact, not stopped; other work on the same key counts against the job, so the key-usage figure is an upper bound and a busy key can refuse a legitimate call; key usage lags, so read it at least two to three minutes after the last call; and there is no provider-side ceiling as a backstop.\n   65       - *Story:* an evaluatordriven job shared a key with other work. The ledger and the key's total never reconciled, and the key's own limit stopped a call partway through. In the doctrine-pointer job the ledger and the key-usage change happened to match to the cent, which was luck, not a guarantee.\n   66   11. **Decide the cross-family pre-read per job, on the approval sheet.** A **pre-read** by a model family not already in the job is **optional**: put it on the **approval sheet** as a numbered item with your recommendation — yes for a new kind of bar or new instruments, usually no for a small repeat of a known shape. If the Owner says yes, the reviewer reads the bar, the runbook and the instruments before freeze and lists what is unclear or unfair; fix or justify every finding in a file committed with the job, and repeat until a round adds nothing new. A pre-read catches wording and gate problems; it cannot catch a starved model, so it never replaces the live run.\n   67       - *Owner decision, 2026-10-28 15:52 EDT:* the pre-read is optional and is chosen per job.\n   68       - *Story:* in the evaluatordriven jobs successive pre-read rounds returned 35, then 38, then 4, then 3, then 0 findings, and the Architect fixed or justified each one.\n   69   12. **Show every referee or checker a malformed input before you trust it on a good one.** Any instrument that settles something — a checker, a tiebreak, a schema validator, a verifier — gets records with missing fields, malformed fields and wrong types first, and must fail them the way the specification says.\n   70       - *Story:* in the evaluatordriven jobs two adjudicators split on a question, and the tiebreak instrument did not implement the spec's rule for missing and malformed fields. It settled the split the wrong way. Our equivalent: each failure type of the verifier gets its own planted case.\n   71   13. **Log every Owner decision as it happens.** Time with zone, channel or message id, the words, and whether you witnessed it or it came relayed. Every decision gets that treatment, not only amendments.\n   72       - *Story:* the evaluatordriven bar capped Owner decisions at 10, the job used 12, and one of the logged decisions could not be tied to any event.\n   73   \n   74   ### Freeze gate\n   75   \n   76   Tick every box before you freeze:\n   77   \n   78   - [ ] Every model-calling instrument ran live at the frozen settings, output attached and its hash cited.\n   79   - [ ] Each task sized to finish inside the model's maximum; `finish_reason` logged on every call.\n   80   - [ ] No throttling: output tokens at the endpoint max from a saved snapshot, and no reasoning limit.\n   81   - [ ] Maker delivers whole files between markers; the runbook names the command that writes them; the diff scope is checked against the allow-list after extraction.\n   82   - [ ] Cap written as \"cap N E-DI iterations; Maker attempts limited by budget, the clock-time bound, and the tripwire\".\n   83   - [ ] Spend enforced in the harness: the pre-call guard on the shared key, baseline set and the refusal path tested before freeze; the record will call key-usage reconciliation an upper bound.\n   84   - [ ] Cross-family pre-read decided on the approval sheet; if yes, every finding fixed or justified in a committed file.\n   85   - [ ] Every referee and checker shown a malformed input and seen to fail it.\n   86   - [ ] Owner decisions logged with time, channel, words and witnessed-or-relayed; approvals planned against the clock and any decision cap.\n   87   - [ ] Instruments and redacted logs, with the full log's hash beside them, planned to be committed beside the work.\n   88   - [ ] Logged files are write-once by content.\n   89   - [ ] Any combined seat drafted as a `separationGaps` line.\n   90   \n   91   ## 4. Running the loop\n   92   \n   93   ### Maker turn\n   94   \n   95   - Hand off with exact preconditions: branch head, ledger hash, prompt file hash, harness hash, log entry count, clock cutoff. If any does not match, the Maker stops. That is a feature. Every dependency outside the repo goes in the preconditions too, with a script that restores and verifies it.\n   96     - *Story:* the tests read an external corpus through a pin file and reached it through a symlink that no handoff had listed. A reboot wiped the symlink and the tests could not run until the cause was found and the link recreated, after the corpus files were checked against the pinned hashes.\n   97   - Give the Maker the Evaluator's findings from the previous iteration — instrument reasons and the model judge's reply — relayed unedited, and redacted of anything from held-out controls. The Maker never sees them.\n   98   - **Verify the Maker's report yourself, and read the diff to say what kind of fix it is.** Re-extract the files from the raw model reply and confirm they are byte-identical to the commit, check the paths against the allow-list, re-run the tests, reconcile spend with key usage. \"The tests pass\" tells you the files fit the harness, not how the finding was fixed. Do not judge it, that is the Evaluator's job, but write it plainly in the generation close line.\n   99     - *Story:* iteration 2 failed on a finding because a repository fixture's custody times were hand-typed with no ledger line behind them. The next generation added ledger events to the fixture carrying the same hand-typed times and relaxed the emitter's cross-checks so the new events would agree. The allow-list included the fixture folder, so it was a legal move and the easiest one. Whether it met the bar was the Evaluator's call, but \"does a fixture-only fix count?\" should have been settled in the bar before the Maker's turn.\n  100   - **When an attempt breaks the tests, run the same tests at the unchanged base commit, in the same environment, before you blame anyone.** If the base passes, the break is the Maker's; if the base fails too, it is your setup. Record the base run as a deviation outside the repo.\n  101     - *Story:* a generation crashed the suite with a missing file, and it was the first run under a new temp root, so the environment was a real suspect. One extra run at the unchanged base, in a throwaway worktree with the same environment, passed everything and settled it. The failure text went back to the model and a later call was accepted.\n  102   - **Ask the Maker to quote the Owner's go-ahead in its report.** You need it for the close line, and you did not see it happen.\n  103     - *Story:* one generation's report covered calls, costs and deviations in detail but not the Owner's yes, so the close line could only say the go-ahead belonged to the Maker's chat and was not witnessed by the Orchestrator.\n  104   - **Diff each filled handoff against the previous one.** A template copied forward carries its old words.\n  105     - *Story:* a batch template still said the previous batch's directory did not exist yet. It was harmless, but a newcomer Evaluator could have checked the wrong folder.\n  106   \n  107   ### When the box itself fails mid-run\n  108   \n  109   - Your machine can reboot under you. On one job it rebooted during a Maker generation: the call died with no exit file and no log entry, the temp area including the test venv was wiped, the scratch worktree vanished, and committed files in an unrelated job folder showed as deleted.\n  110   - What to do: do not panic and do not re-run anything yet; check what is damaged against git and re-verify the ledger and harness hashes; restore committed files from git, never from memory; record the lost call as lost, with what you know, and never invent a log entry for it; resume from the last good state.\n  111   - **Never put job work in `/tmp`.** `/tmp` is wiped on reboot.\n  112     - *Story:* the handoffs built the test venv under `/tmp`, the Maker ran its calls from wrapper scripts there, and follow-up checks wrote their JSON there. The reboot took all of it, and the Maker had to rebuild before it could resume. Put venvs, wrapper scripts, scratch worktrees and intermediate files under a job work root that survives a reboot, and name that path in every handoff.\n  113   - **Moving your own paths is not enough; move the tools' temp files too. Set `TMPDIR`.**\n  114     - *Story:* after the reboot the handoff moved every named path to a persistent root, but nobody told the tests where to put their own temp files. The Maker caught it during pre-checks, created a per-seat temp folder under the work root, exported `TMPDIR` to it, and only then ran anything; one test script alone would have left 32 folders in `/tmp`. Every handoff exports `TMPDIR` per seat and per run, and close lists and cleans those folders.\n  115   - **Record exact tool and venv versions at job open.**\n  116     - *Story:* the venv was rebuilt after a reboot and nothing written at job open said what the original held, so \"same as before\" could only be assumed. The identical test output was the only evidence the rebuild matched.\n  117   \n  118   ### Evaluator turn\n  119   \n  120   - Give the Evaluator a **writable** copy of the controls and check integrity with the manifest before and after.\n  121     - *Story:* the copy was made read-only to protect it. The scoring harness copies fixtures with their permissions, so the subject's emitter could not write its output and five checks failed for environmental reasons. The batch was void.\n  122   - Read key usage at least two to three minutes after the last call, or the last call's cost will not show.\n  123     - *Story:* one batch waited about 156 seconds after the last call, and only then did the after-read reconcile to the cent; the earlier read had under-counted.\n  124   - When results look strange, **ask \"is this the subject or my setup?\"** before applying the pass rule. A void batch is recorded as void, with its causes; it is not a score. Even in a void batch, look for real signal: in one void batch two checks failed on a fixture the setup bug had not touched, and that result held in the next batch.\n  125   - **Read which call is the subject from the batch's own order.** Control order rotates per batch; the verdict, the evidence copy and the close inputs all take the subject's call name from the batch record, not from a position. To check that the subject prompt was sent verbatim, rebuild it exactly as the harness does, numbered listing included — a plain file dump will not match byte for byte even when nothing is wrong.\n  126     - *Story:* in one batch the subject ran first in the order rather than second as in earlier batches, so anything keyed to the earlier position would have judged the wrong call.\n  127   \n  128   ### Applying the pass rule\n  129   \n  130   - Mechanical: every dimension must pass. Report what failed, quote the instrument's reason in plain words, and say what it means for the work.\n  131   - At the cap the state is `needs owner`. Bring the Owner the options (amend, reject) with your recommendation and the clock arithmetic for each.\n  132   \n  133   ## 5. Records\n  134   \n  135   - The job record lives in the product repo beside the code, under `dot-plumbline/jobs/<job_id>/` (doctrine rule 12). Not in scratch.\n  136   - **Commit the instruments and the redacted logs beside the work, not just the record.** A stranger can only check what the clone contains.\n  137     - *Story:* in one evaluatordriven job the repo carried the record but not the job log, the evidence files or the acceptance instruments, and a public check of the clone read NOT-CHECKABLE on two requirements the job had been calling expected. Redacted logs plus the full log's hash is the fix.\n  138   - The ledger is append-only. Every amendment, generation open and close, and evaluation verdict gets a line; record the ledger hash after each append and pin it in the next handoff. The ledger tool could not yet write generation, evaluation or amendment lines, so they were hand-appended, and that is disclosed.\n  139   - **Keep logged files write-once, by content.** Before an event cites a file, copy it to `evidence/<name>-<hash8>.<ext>` and cite the copy. A correction is a new file and a new event, never an edit. At close, run the log's file check and say which cited files no longer match and why.\n  140     - *Story:* while drafting the custody-times job we found that the emitter rewrites evidence logs in place, filling send and receive times from the server's clock, and that running the tests changes two committed fixture logs. Content-named, write-once evidence would have made that change visible as a mismatch instead of a silent edit.\n  141   - Post-freeze amendments go beside the frozen set, never inside `custody/`, so the bar digest stays valid. Each one records the Owner's words, with time, channel, and whether you witnessed it.\n  142   - Model-judge logs are committed redacted — requests and replies, since replies can quote control code — with the full log's hash alongside. The full log stays outside the repo. Held-out control files are digested from where they sit on the box; the record carries their hash only, and it says that a stranger cannot re-read them.\n  143   - The provider key is referenced by path only. Scan for it before every push.\n  144   - Git identity: author `E-DI Maker` on Maker commits, committer the Owner's recorded identity, never a helper's.\n  145   \n  146   ### Preparing the close\n  147   \n  148   - Close runs from a clean checkout of base main with the expected revision pinned, not from the branch under test.\n  149   - A tool that stamps the time it runs, not the time the Owner decided, needs the decision time written into the input and a matching ledger line by hand. Disclose the hand line; the emitter refuses if the two disagree.\n  150   - Dry-run the whole close, commit included, on a scratch branch worktree under the job work root. Test both dispositions and check that the pre-checks refuse bad input. The dry run is what finds the record's own defects: on one job the first dry run failed a conformance check because the instrument's runner and the script evaluator were the same script, and the fix was to name the written procedure as the runner.\n  151   \n  152   ### After the close and the merge\n  153   \n  154   - **Take every time floor from the log itself, never from a summary of it.**\n  155     - *Story:* a first floor was set from batch notes, and the dry-run record showed a check finishing a minute later. The harness log held the real last time, so the floor moved to it. With the wrong floor, a decision time in that minute would have dated the Owner's yes before the check it was approving.\n  156   - **Have the close ready before the Owner decides.** On one job the disposition, the close commit and the merge all landed inside two minutes, because every surprise had already happened in the dry run.\n  157   - **After the merge the job branch is finished.** Later changes to the job record go on a new branch cut from the merge commit.\n  158     - *Story:* the final sync of this guide came after the job's pull request was merged. It went on a fresh branch cut from the merge commit and touched only the guide; commits added to the merged job branch would have been stranded off main.\n  159   - **A merge changes the next job's starting point. Re-read the pins before drafting; do not copy the previous sheet's pins.**\n  160   \n  161   ## 6. Working with the Owner\n  162   \n  163   - The Owner decides; you recommend. Lead with the answer, give your recommendation, and offer real options. Show the clock arithmetic when time matters.\n  164   - When the Owner gives a principle — don't starve a model, the cap counts loop iterations — apply it everywhere it fits, not only where it was said, and write it down here.\n  165   - Own your mistakes plainly. \"I froze an 800-token cap\" beats explaining.\n  166   - Push only on the Owner's authority, and cite the message.\n  167   - **When a job touches a live site, check pages with a browser-style `Accept` header.**\n  168     - *Story:* in the evaluatordriven jobs a bare request missed a script a CDN injected only into HTML requests. We have no story of our own yet.\n  169   \n  170   ## 7. Carried forward\n  171   \n  172   Short list of what was still open when this version was written:\n  173   \n  174   - **Fixture-only fixes.** Is changing a fixture an acceptable fix for a finding about that fixture? Settle it in the bar and the allow-list before the Maker's first turn. The proposal on the table is no: a fixture or test change never counts as the fix for a finding.\n  175   - **Fields the emitter cannot carry.** An emitter with no pass-through for extra record fields forced the spend line and a limitation note into a sidecar file that carries the record's hash. Adopt the sidecar as the rule, or add the pass-through.\n  176   - **One record per iteration?** Only the final iteration of one job got a full custody record; earlier iterations live in verdict files and the ledger. Decide whether every iteration gets one.\n  177   - **Follow-up work:** make the emitter refuse or flag hand-entered times, add ledger commands for generation, evaluation, amendment and dispose-at-decision-time, fixture modes, and spend caps enforced in the harness.\n  178   - **Fold this guide into the skill and the runbook.** Re-read it after the next job: a rule with no story behind it is a candidate for deletion.\n"
