and discloses it.\n\nBut is that in scope for the check? The check's RULE C: \"The doctrine below... The guide may add practice, but nothing it says may contradict the doctrine.\" So hand-appending ledger lines contradicts doctrine principle 12's \"never retyped by hand\"? Let me think carefully.\n\nDoctrine 12: \"Evidence or it did not happen. Every claim cites a file, kept by its content hash. Notes are written from what the instruments produced, never from what someone intended. Keep the instrument that accepted the work, and the record, with the work: committed beside it, not in local scratch. Pin every standard by hash or commit, never by a version name alone; a version number cannot be checked. Figures, hashes and record fields are printed by the tool that produced them, never retyped by hand.\"\n\nThe guide line 138: \"The ledger is append-only. Every amendment, generation open and close, and evaluation verdict gets a line; record the ledger hash after each append and pin it in the next handoff. The ledger tool could not yet write generation, evaluation or amendment lines, so they were hand-appended, and that is disclosed.\"\n\nAlso line 149: \"A tool that stamps the time it runs, not the time the Owner decided, needs the decision time written into the input and a matching ledger line by hand. Disclose the hand line; the emitter refuses if the two disagree.\"\n\nHmm. These describe deviations that were disclosed. Is disclosure enough? The doctrine's principle is \"never retyped by hand\". The guide describes hand-appended lines as an accepted practice with disclosure. A reader following the guide would hand-append ledger lines when the tool lacks the capability — acting against doctrine 12's \"never retyped by hand\".\n\nBut hold on — the question's RULE focuses primarily on A1-A3, B1-B9, and C. The check says: \"The document must not contradict, weaken or misstate any of the following, and must not claim to override the doctrine.\" The \"following\" includes C: the doctrine. So doctrine contradictions count.\n\nHowever, the instruction also says: \"A statement contradicts a rule if a reader following the document would act against the rule. Leaving out a rule is not a contradiction for this check (presence is checked elsewhere); stating its opposite is.\"\n\nSo for C, does the guide state the opposite of a doctrine rule? Hand-appending ledger lines... The doctrine says figures/hashes/record fields are printed by the tool that produced them, never retyped by hand. The guide's practice of hand-appending ledger lines is arguably retyping by hand. But is it \"figures, hashes and record fields\"? A ledger line for generation open/close includes fields. The guide says they were hand-appended and disclosed. Disclosure is the doctrine's own mechanism for combined seats (seats may share if disclosed). For evidence, the doctrine doesn't have a disclosure exception for hand-typing.\n\nHmm, but is this the kind of thing the check is designed to catch? The check seems focused on the specific decisions (A1-A3) and standing rules (B1-B9) and general doctrine conformance (C). The task says \"for CONFORMS, the lines that state A1, A2 and A3\" — suggesting the expected answer is likely CONFORMS with lines 29, 62, 66 (or similar). The check is likely designed such that the document conforms. But I need to actually evaluate, not guess.\n\nLet me re-scan the document for anything that contradicts A, B, or C.\n\nPotential issues:\n\n1. Line 3: \"Nothing here overrides any of them, and where this guide and the doctrine disagree, the doctrine wins.\" — satisfies the \"doctrine wins on conflict\" requirement.\n\n2. Line 27: \"Iteration = one turn of the loop: the Maker produces, the Evaluator scores, you apply the pass rule. The cap counts E-DI iterations. The Owner settled this on 2026-10-28: the three meant three turns of the loop, not three Maker sessions.\" — consistent with B3 and A1.\n\n3. Line 29: tripwire, exactly 3, same failure class, stop retrying, change request. Consistent with A1. \"A different failure class starts its own count.\" Consistent.\n\nWait — A1 says \"when the same Maker failure class occurs 3 times (exactly three, not 'a few' or another number)\". Line 29 says \"comes up 3 times\". Good.\n\n4. Line 30: \"The tripwire does not cap spend; the budget does.\" Fine.\n\n5. Line 47: B1. \"Stop at the first one... Change the request.\" Good. Also says \"Do not wait for the tripwire's third time\" — consistent.\n\n6. Line 61-64: A2. Good. \"This guide does not recommend a per-job key\" — explicitly satisfies A2's constraint.\n\n7. Line 66-68: A3. \"optional\", \"chosen per job\", \"on the approval sheet\". Good.\n\n8. Line 111-114: B9. \"Never put job work in /tmp.\" Good. But note line 113-114: \"Moving your own paths is not enough; move the tools' temp files too. Set TMPDIR.\" — that's about not using /tmp at all; consistent.\n\nWait, line 111: \"**Never put job work in `/tmp`.** `/tmp` is wiped on reboot.\" — B9 satisfied.\n\n9. Line 55-56: B5. \"Check every handoff against the standing rules before you send it.\" Good.\n\n10. Line 53: B4. Good.\n\n11. Line 49: B6. Good.\n\n12. Line 88: \"Logged files are write-once by content.\" Line 139: B7. Good.\n\n13. Line 17: B8. Good.\n\nNow doctrine-level checks:\n\n- The guide says the Orchestrator \"write[s] the bar with the Owner\" (line 13). Doctrine: Architect writes the standard; Orchestrator never writes the work or scores it. Is writing the bar \"writing the work\"? The \"work\" is the subject work being judged, not the bar. The Architect seat writes the standard. One person may hold several seats if disclosed. The guide explicitly discusses disclosing combined seats (line 21-23). So this is not a contradiction; it's a seat combination that the guide says must be disclosed.\n\n- Line 98: \"**Verify the Maker's report yourself...** Do not judge it, that is the Evaluator's job, but write it plainly in the generation close line.\" — consistent.\n\n- Line 124: \"When results look strange, ask 'is this the subject or my setup?' before applying the pass rule. A void batch is recorded as void... it is not a score.\" — consistent with doctrine (no observation is a finding about the instrument, never a zero for the work).\n\n- Line 130: \"Mechanical: every dimension must pass.\" Consistent.\n\n- Line 131: \"At the cap the state is `needs owner`. Bring the Owner the options (amend, reject) with your recommendation...\" Consistent with doctrine exits (needs owner; Owner may amend; disposition beside verdict).\n\n- Line 139: write-once evidence. Consistent with B7 and doctrine 12.\n\n- Line 141: \"Post-freeze amendments go beside the frozen set, never inside `custody/`, so the bar digest stays valid. Each one records the Owner's words, with time, channel, and whether you witnessed it.\" — consistent with doctrine (verdict stays; decisions written beside).\n\n- Line 144: \"Git identity: author `E-DI Maker` on Maker commits, committer the Owner's recorded identity, never a helper's.\" — fine.\n\n- Line 148-150: close prep. Fine.\n\n- Line 164: \"When the Owner gives a principle — don't starve a model, the cap counts loop iterations — apply it everywhere it fits, not only where it was said, and write it down here.\" Fine.\n\n- Line 17: relayed approvals — matches B8.\n\nNow, does anything misstate A1? A1: \"Maker attempts are still not iterations.\" Line 28 says exactly that. Good.\n\nDoes anything weaken A2? Line 62: \"the job keeps the shared key, guarded before every paid call, rather than taking a per-job key... This guide does not recommend a per-job key; the Owner weighed that option and chose the shared key.\" Good. Line 63: \"What the guard covers: 'spent' is the larger of the job's own ledger and the key's usage change since the job's baseline...\" Wait — \"the larger of the job's own ledger and the key's usage change since the job's baseline\" — this is the guard's spend definition. Because the key is shared, other work on the key counts against the job (conservative), so using the larger is conservative. Fine.\n\nHmm, one thing: line 63 says \"it refuses when key usage cannot be read\" — fine.\n\nDoes anything contradict B2? Line 41: \"No throttling. No reasoning limit, and max output tokens at the endpoint max.\" Good. But wait — line 42 story: \"a Maker generation ran under a 24k token cap.\" That's a story of failure, not a recommendation. Fine.\n\nLine 45: \"It failed five times with finish_reason = length... at about $0.59 a call — roughly $3 before anyone changed the request. Batching fixed it.\" This describes retrying 5 times before changing — a failure story, and B1 says stop at first one. The story describes what went wrong (they retried five times), which is presented as the lesson (\"the 12-record call above was retried five times before anyone resized it\" — line 48, presented as a story of a miss). That's consistent with B1 as the rule is stated as \"Stop at the first one.\" The story illustrates the failure. Not a contradiction.\n\nDoes anything contradict the doctrine about averaging? Line 69-70: referee/checker malformed input. Fine.\n\nLine 59: \"Every Maker or Evaluator model run needs the Owner's yes.\" Hmm, does this contradict the doctrine's human-at-the-helm? No — it adds more human oversight, which is allowed (\"The mistake is not keeping a human too long\").\n\nActually wait, is there tension with \"The Owner's attention is a budget\" and F5? The doctrine lists F5 as a failure signal: \"The Owner is asked questions the system could have answered itself... repeated approvals of the same kind\". The guide requiring Owner approval for every model run could be seen as encouraging F5. But the guide also has the story about a standing ruling to save asks. And the guide's line 59 says \"Plan the approval checkpoints against the clock, and against any decision cap.\" It's about planning approvals. This is practice, not contradiction. The doctrine doesn't mandate fewer approvals; it warns that repeated approvals are a failure signal. Hmm. But the check says \"A statement contradicts a rule if a reader following the document would act against the rule.\" F5 is a failure signal, not a rule a reader acts against per se. And the guide doesn't state the opposite of F5; it actually includes mitigations. I'll not flag.\n\nWhat about line 21: \"Disclose a combined seat. If one seat writes an instrument and also runs the gate it is judged by, or applies the pass rule to its own instrument's output, say so in the record's `separationGaps`.\" — consistent with doctrine §3 (disclosure).\n\nDoctrine: \"the Maker never judges its own work\" — never waived. The guide doesn't violate that.\n\nDoctrine: Evaluator must not be biased toward Maker's family — the guide's cross-family pre-read (A3) is about a pre-read by another family, optional. The doctrine §3 requires at least one Evaluator to differ from the Maker in model family. The guide's optional pre-read is a different thing (pre-read of instruments before freeze, not the Evaluator seat). Does the guide's statement \"the pre-read is optional\" contradict the doctrine's family-bias rule? The pre-read is described as \"A pre-read by a model family not already in the job\" — reading the bar, runbook, instruments before freeze. The doctrine's requirement is about the Evaluator seat family composition, not about pre-reads. §6: \"Have someone independent pre-read the instruments. The Architect writes the runbook and the scorer; an adjudicator reads both against the bar before any work starts.\" Hmm! Doctrine §6 says \"Have someone independent pre-read the instruments\" — that seems to be a standing qualification practice in the doctrine. The guide says the cross-family pre-read is optional, decided per job.\n\nWait, is the \"cross-family pre-read\" the same as the doctrine's \"independent pre-read\"? The doctrine §6: \"Have someone independent pre-read the instruments. The Architect writes the runbook and the scorer; an adjudicator reads both against the bar before any work starts. Where the instrument and the bar differ, the bar governs.\"\n\nThe guide (A3 decision): \"the cross-family pre-read is optional, decided per job on the approval sheet (not mandatory, not dropped).\"\n\nHmm, this is interesting. The Owner's decision A3 makes the cross-family pre-read optional. The doctrine §6 says \"Have someone independent pre-read the instruments.\" Is the doctrine's pre-read required to be cross-family? The doctrine says \"someone independent\" — an adjudicator. The guide's pre-read is specifically \"by a model family not already in the job\". These might be different things: the doctrine's independent pre-read could be by a person (an adjudicator is an Evaluator who rules on disagreements...). Actually §6's \"someone independent pre-read the instruments\" — in practice, an adjudicator reads both against the bar.\n\nThe check's RULE A3 states: \"The cross-family pre-read is optional, decided per job on the approval sheet (not mandatory, not dropped).\" So the rule I'm checking against explicitly says optional. The guide conforms to A3. And does making the cross-family pre-read optional contradict doctrine §6's \"Have someone independent pre-read the instruments\"? The doctrine's pre-read is \"someone independent\" — not necessarily cross-family model. The guide's line 66 says the pre-read \"never replaces the live run\" and is about wording/gate problems. The guide doesn't say \"don't do independent pre-reads\"; it says the cross-family pre-read is optional per job. Given A3 is the Owner's decision and the rule states it as such, the guide conforms. The doctrine's §6 pre-read by \"someone independent\" — hmm, if the cross-family pre-read is dropped for a job, is there still an independent pre-read? The doctrine's §6 practice might still be satisfied by the Owner or an adjudicator reading. The guide doesn't state that no pre-read happens;