ry at line 65. ✓ (directly under)\n   3. Cross-family pre-read (line 66) — story at line 68. ✓ (directly under)\n   4. finish_reason length empty content (line 47) — story at line 48. ✓ (directly under)\n   5. No throttling (line 41) — stories at 42-43. Sized tasks + finish_reason logged (line 44) — story at 45-46. ✓ (directly under; rule 5 spans both items, each with story)\n   6. Cap counts E-DI iterations (line 27) — story? The nearest story is line 33 under the \"write the cap\" bullet. Not directly under line 27. Hmm.\n   7. Handoffs name the command that writes files (line 53) — story at line 54. ✓\n   8. Check handoffs against standing rules (line 55) — story at line 56. ✓\n   9. Live test of each model instrument is a freeze gate (line 49) — story at line 50. ✓\n   10. Write-once evidence (line 139) — story at line 140. ✓\n   11. Relayed approvals are not approvals (line 17) — story at line 19. ✓\n   12. No /tmp (line 111) — story at line 112. ✓\n\n   So the only questionable one is rule 6.\n\n   Let me look at line 27 again: \"- **Iteration** = one turn of the loop: the Maker produces, the Evaluator scores, you apply the pass rule. **The cap counts E-DI iterations.** The Owner settled this on 2026-10-28: the three meant three turns of the loop, not three Maker sessions.\"\n\n   \"The Owner settled this on 2026-10-28: the three meant three turns of the loop, not three Maker sessions.\" — This references an incident where the meaning of \"the three\" was in question. But is it a story? It says what the Owner decided, not what happened and what it cost. It's more like a ruling than a story. Hmm.\n\n   But then the story at line 33: is it \"placed with the rule\"? The rule \"the cap counts E-DI iterations\" is the headline of line 27. The story at line 33 is under a related rule. The RULE states: \"placed with the rule (directly under it or in the same item). One story may serve two rules only if it is told with both.\"\n\n   Hmm, \"told with both\" — if the story at line 33 is told with the \"write the cap in the bar\" rule, and that rule is arguably a restatement/application of rule 6... The question is whether the story serves rule 6.\n\n   Let me consider the counter-argument: the \"write the cap in the bar\" rule is a distinct rule (about phrasing the cap in the bar), and its story is about the phrasing problem. But the story's content is also the story of rule 6: what happened when the cap was read as counting generations rather than iterations. The story says \"the bar said the attempts were gone\" when iterations failed for plumbing reasons — i.e., the cap counted Maker sessions/generations rather than E-DI iterations.\n\n   Actually hold on. Let me re-read: \"our bar said 'cap 3 generations'. Generations 1 and 2 failed for plumbing reasons (a token cap, malformed diffs) before the Evaluator scored anything, and when iteration 1 then failed scoring the bar said the attempts were gone.\"\n\n   So the bar counted \"generations\" (Maker attempts) — three generations were used up by plumbing failures, so when iteration 1 failed scoring, no attempts remained. This is the incident that led to \"the cap counts E-DI iterations.\" Yes, this is the story of rule 6.\n\n   And it's in the same section, same list, two bullets below. Hmm.\n\n   The strict reading of the RULE: \"placed with the rule (directly under it or in the same item)\". The story at line 33 is not directly under line 27, nor in the same item (line 27's bullet ends at line 27). So strictly, rule 6's story is not \"placed with\" the rule as stated at line 27.\n\n   But wait — is the rule \"the cap counts E-DI iterations\" perhaps also stated at line 32 (\"Write the cap in the bar in one sentence: 'cap N E-DI iterations; ...'\")? Line 32 quotes the cap sentence which includes \"cap N E-DI iterations\". Hmm, that's the practice that encodes rule 6. If we consider line 32 to be a statement of rule 6 (as well as a phrasing rule), then the story at line 33 is directly under it. \n\n   Hmm, this is genuinely ambiguous. Let me think about the purpose of the question. The question seems designed to test whether the evaluator can check each of the 12 rules for a paired story. The document seems deliberately constructed so that most rules have stories. Rule 6 is the tricky one.\n\n   Let me look at the exact wording of line 27 again: \"**The cap counts E-DI iterations.** The Owner settled this on 2026-10-28: the three meant three turns of the loop, not three Maker sessions.\"\n\n   Compare with how other rules' stories read: \"in the custody-times job...\", \"in the v0.9.6 job 9 of 10 Maker attempts failed...\". The story elements: what happened (a concrete incident), and what it cost or showed.\n\n   Line 27's \"The Owner settled this on 2026-10-28\" — this is a decision record, not a story. It doesn't say what happened at any job or what it cost.\n\n   Line 33's story is the story. The question is placement.\n\n   Consider the alternative: maybe the intended answer is CONFORMS, with the reasoning that the story at line 33 is the story of the cap-counts-iterations rule and it's in the same list/section, arguably \"in the same item\" if we consider the \"write the cap in the bar\" item as part of the cap rule. Hmm.\n\n   Or maybe the intended answer is DOES_NOT_CONFORM with rule 6 (line 27) flagged.\n\n   Let me re-read the RULE once more: \"Every one of the rules listed here that the document states must be paired with its real story: a concrete incident from a real job or review (what happened, and what it cost or showed), placed with the rule (directly under it or in the same item). A bare reason, a hypothetical ('if you did X, Y could happen'), or a pointer elsewhere is not a story.\"\n\n   \"A pointer elsewhere is not a story\" — this refers to the story content being a pointer, not the placement. The placement requirement: \"directly under it or in the same item.\"\n\n   For rule 6 at line 27: the story is at line 33, which